[test-suite name=llm-cli ring=1
  [doc [#
# CLI invocation cases the guide cites (RULED: DOCSOK-1, DOCS-51 §4, RS-30)

The executable backing for the guide's examples that ARE a shell invocation
of `cx` — the FAQ's `cx hash`, `cx fmt`, `cx diff`, `cx lint`, the JSON
projection and the one-liner. A guide page cites a case here with
`[fixture id=…]` and shows the invocation and its LIVE output, the way the
primer's console blocks do: `scripts/gen_docs/primer_build.cx` replays every
cited case against the running `cx` when the guide is built, and a case whose
output moved stops the build, naming it (`make guide`, `make guide-check`).

Why a suite of its own, beside `antipatterns.cxd`: these cases are not
wrong/right pairs, and an invocation-level case has no expression in the
subject corpora of cx-core-data (`identity_hash.cxd`, `fmt.cxd`, `diff.cxd`,
`lint.cxd`), whose grading cores compare the verb's own result, not what the
command prints. Those suites stay the normative grade of each verb; a case
here pins the one invocation a reader copies. Like `antipatterns.cxd`, it
lives under `conformance/llm/` so the top-level corpus scanners keep their
pinned baselines, and it is graded by the replay, not by a corpus shard.

## Format

The `antipatterns.cxd` vocabulary: `[in-cx]` (the document; `[empty]` for
none), `[in-code]` (the program, or the second document of a two-file verb),
`[cli-argv]` (one argv token per line; `%DATA%` = the `[in-cx]` file, shown
as `input.cx`; `%FILE%` = the `[in-code]` file, shown as `prog.cx`; `%CODE%`
= the `[in-code]` body as one token), `[out-text]` (stdout when non-empty,
else stderr, byte-exact after edge newlines), `[exit-code]`.
#]]

 [; ── the one-liner: the first thing to run once cx is on PATH ── ]

 [case id=cli-e-one-liner level=core
  [title [#an inline program with -e#]]
  [tags cli run-surface expression faq]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[+ 1 2]
#]]
  [cli-argv [#
-e
%CODE%
#]]
  [out-text [#
3
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [; ── identity: the hash is over the strict-canonical bytes ── ]

 [case id=cli-hash-presentation-a level=core
  [title [#cx hash over a document with a comment, double quotes and a line break#]]
  [tags cli hash identity canonical presentation faq]
  [in-cx [#
[; the service, as one team wrote it]
[server   host="localhost"
        port=8080]
#]]
  [cli-argv [#
hash
%DATA%
#]]
  [out-text [#
sha2-256:d8ff354eba4f49456ef0d364f18ef3d772a4faab862da34d49f3ea40172f2825
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [case id=cli-hash-presentation-b level=core
  [title [#cx hash over the same document written tidily — the same digest#]]
  [tags cli hash identity canonical presentation faq]
  [in-cx [#
[server host=localhost port=8080]
#]]
  [cli-argv [#
hash
%DATA%
#]]
  [out-text [#
sha2-256:d8ff354eba4f49456ef0d364f18ef3d772a4faab862da34d49f3ea40172f2825
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [case id=cli-hash-attribute-order level=core
  [title [#cx hash with the two attributes swapped — a different digest#]]
  [tags cli hash identity canonical attribute-order faq]
  [in-cx [#
[server port=8080 host=localhost]
#]]
  [cli-argv [#
hash
%DATA%
#]]
  [out-text [#
sha2-256:2ea0b5acdae2502d7d0c6b85796f7c0e397ccf0a9ad2126b0f72af57a6330aab
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [; ── attribute order is data: fmt and the JSON projection keep it ── ]

 [case id=cli-fmt-keeps-attribute-order level=core
  [title [#cx fmt tidies spacing and quotes and keeps the authored attribute order#]]
  [tags cli fmt attribute-order faq]
  [in-cx [#
[server   port=8080  host="localhost"]
#]]
  [cli-argv [#
fmt
%DATA%
#]]
  [out-text [#
[server port=8080 host=localhost]
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [case id=cli-json-keeps-attribute-order level=core
  [title [#the JSON projection keeps the authored attribute order#]]
  [tags cli conversion json attribute-order faq]
  [in-cx [#
[server port=8080 host=localhost]
#]]
  [cli-argv [#
--from=cx
--to=json
%DATA%
#]]
  [out-text [#
{
  "server": {
    "port": 8080,
    "host": "localhost"
  }
}
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [; ── the semantic diff: presentation is no change, a value is one ── ]

 [case id=cli-diff-semantic level=core
  [title [#cx diff reports the one changed value and nothing of the re-quoting#]]
  [tags cli diff canonical faq]
  [in-cx [#
[server host=localhost port=8080]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[; re-quoted and re-spaced; one value changed]
[server host="acme.com"   port=8080]
#]]
  [cli-argv [#
diff
%DATA%
%FILE%
#]]
  [out-text [#
@@ /server/@host @@
- host=localhost
+ host=acme.com
#]]
  [exit-code [#1#]]
 ]

 [; ── lint: one file's findings; a warning is below the default --fail-on ── ]

 [case id=cli-lint-unused-anchor level=core
  [title [#cx lint names an anchor nothing references#]]
  [tags cli lint l003 faq]
  [in-cx [#
[base &b host=localhost]
[other host=acme.com]
#]]
  [cli-argv [#
lint
%DATA%
#]]
  [out-text [#
warn CX-L003: anchor 'b' declared but never referenced [/base]
 suggestion: remove the anchor declaration, or add a *b reference
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]

 [; ── deny-by-default: an include with no read grant stays unresolved ── ]

 [case id=cli-include-ungranted-unresolved level=core
  [title [#with no --allow-read an include directive is left as it is, unresolved#]]
  [tags cli include capability deny-by-default faq]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[config [?cx include='../secrets.cx'] [app name=demo]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[config [?cx include=../secrets.cx] [app name=demo]]
#]]
  [exit-code [#0#]]
 ]
]
